To configure basic SSH access on a Cisco IOS router or switch, you need to fulfill a few global prerequisites (like setting a hostname and domain name) 
before generating the encryption keys and forcing the virtual lines to accept SSH.


Step-by-Step ConfigurationEnter global configuration mode first:

Router> enable
Router# configure terminal





Cisco IOS uses the device hostname and IP domain name together to name the cryptographic keys.

Router(config)# hostname R1
R1(config)# ip domain-name yourdomain.com





Create a Local User Account - You need a local account to log in via SSH. Using secret instead of password ensures the password is encrypted 
with a strong hash inside the configuration.

R1(config)# username admin privilege 15 secret YourSecurePassword





Generate the RSA key pairs that will encrypt your SSH traffic.

crypto key generate rsa
! input--> 2048



By default, Cisco IOS may fall back to the older, insecure SSH version 1. 
Force the device to use version 2 only

ip ssh version 2




Configure the Virtual Terminal (VTY) lines to check the local user database for authentication, and block insecure Telnet traffic entirely.

R1(config)# line vty 0 4
R1(config-line)# login local
R1(config-line)# transport input ssh
R1(config-line)# exit



Check SSH status: Validate if the server is running and see what version is active.

show ip ssh




Confirm that your 2048-bit RSA key was created successfully.

R1# show crypto key mypubkey rsa




You can test connecting from another Cisco device using the built-in client:

Router# ssh -l admin <IP_ADDRESS_OF_HOST>





